WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.076 --> 00:00:02.555
- Hey, Texas, yip, yip!

00:00:06.575 --> 00:00:08.866
So when we say "Southern Style Barbecue,"

00:00:08.866 --> 00:00:10.532
it's a lot of different regions that

00:00:10.532 --> 00:00:12.413
have very specific styles, right?

00:00:12.413 --> 00:00:14.465
But in general, the main
thing you gotta know

00:00:14.465 --> 00:00:15.872
is oh fucking delicious.

00:00:15.872 --> 00:00:17.144
- All you can eat Korean
barbecue you get like

00:00:17.144 --> 00:00:19.399
a lot of side dishes and stuff, but like

00:00:19.399 --> 00:00:20.313
I feel like the main focus

00:00:20.313 --> 00:00:21.573
for this is like the meat.

00:00:21.573 --> 00:00:22.954
- I'm vegetarian so I'm just going

00:00:22.954 --> 00:00:25.351
to be eating everything but the meat.

00:00:25.351 --> 00:00:27.073
- I'm here to eat, you know what I mean?

00:00:27.073 --> 00:00:27.948
I'm hungry.

00:00:27.948 --> 00:00:28.539
- I'm ready for it.

00:00:28.539 --> 00:00:30.144
See, I get so turned on by meat, it's bad.

00:00:30.144 --> 00:00:31.993
Sausage, these are hot links!

00:00:31.993 --> 00:00:33.345
- Should I--
- Yeah, go for it.

00:00:33.345 --> 00:00:36.067
- I feel like it's not
gonna be that spicy.

00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:38.526
- The skin is really tough
which I'm not used to.

00:00:38.526 --> 00:00:39.867
- What are you used to, like hot dogs?

00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:40.923
- Yeah.

00:00:40.923 --> 00:00:43.118
- Hot dogs are supposed
to be like soft and chewy.

00:00:43.118 --> 00:00:44.113
This is kinda crunchy.

00:00:44.113 --> 00:00:45.373
- That was good.
- Yeah?

00:00:45.373 --> 00:00:46.754
- Yeah.
- I believe you.

00:00:46.754 --> 00:00:48.644
- That's actually pretty
spicy, I'm impressed.

00:00:48.644 --> 00:00:49.944
- They're not like super spicy.

00:00:49.944 --> 00:00:52.687
As a Korean person, I'm used
to like super spicy food.

00:00:52.687 --> 00:00:54.109
- Yeah, I approve of this, this is good.

00:00:54.109 --> 00:00:55.308
- Oh, you like it!

00:00:55.308 --> 00:00:55.941
- Yeah.
- Yeah!

00:00:55.941 --> 00:00:57.282
Alright, we're gonna go to the brisket.

00:00:57.282 --> 00:00:59.882
Now as a Texan, brisket
is for me where it's at.

00:00:59.882 --> 00:01:00.776
- Do you want some of this?

00:01:00.776 --> 00:01:01.650
- Oh, I'm OK, thank you.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:02.605
- What's a brisket?

00:01:02.605 --> 00:01:04.454
- It's a cow chest, the cow pecs.

00:01:04.454 --> 00:01:05.409
It's like (grunts).

00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:07.057
- It just looks like a piece of bark

00:01:07.057 --> 00:01:09.414
that fell off a tree.
- (laughing) It does.

00:01:09.414 --> 00:01:11.446
- (mumbles) bite into it, stop cutting.

00:01:11.446 --> 00:01:13.155
- You can have that, I'll have this.

00:01:13.155 --> 00:01:14.191
- I'm enjoying this.

00:01:14.191 --> 00:01:15.939
- I'm afraid to open my
mouth now 'cause I have

00:01:15.939 --> 00:01:17.808
like a piece of meat like--

00:01:17.808 --> 00:01:18.905
- [Woman] I see it.

00:01:18.905 --> 00:01:20.957
- Definitely gotta bring
my floss for this one.

00:01:20.957 --> 00:01:22.115
- Do we put barbecue sauce on this?

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:25.082
- Some barbecue styles come with

00:01:25.082 --> 00:01:26.016
sauce smothered on top

00:01:26.016 --> 00:01:27.656
and some come with it on the side.

00:01:27.656 --> 00:01:29.301
I'm from Texas so I do it from the side.

00:01:29.301 --> 00:01:31.658
From what I understand then, you've never

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:32.999
had barbecue ribs.

00:01:32.999 --> 00:01:34.198
- I feel like I've had some from

00:01:34.198 --> 00:01:35.458
like Outback Steakhouse.

00:01:35.458 --> 00:01:36.636
- Oh (beep) no!

00:01:36.636 --> 00:01:38.241
- I feel like the true test of this is how

00:01:38.241 --> 00:01:39.826
well the meat falls off the bone.

00:01:39.826 --> 00:01:41.065
- Oh, it just comes right off.

00:01:41.065 --> 00:01:42.386
- That's a good sign, right?

00:01:42.386 --> 00:01:43.666
- Yeah, that's a great sign.

00:01:43.666 --> 00:01:45.271
- Mm.
- That looks so good.

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:46.002
- It is so good.

00:01:46.002 --> 00:01:46.876
- You know what animal this is, right?

00:01:46.876 --> 00:01:48.543
- This is pig meat, right?

00:01:48.543 --> 00:01:50.474
- Cow?
- No, pig, you idiot!

00:01:50.474 --> 00:01:51.813
- This is like love at first bite.

00:01:51.813 --> 00:01:53.283
This is like really good.

00:01:53.283 --> 00:01:54.543
- That's really tender.

00:01:54.543 --> 00:01:55.644
- That's really good.
- Oh my god.

00:01:55.644 --> 00:01:56.711
- Now you got two of 'em.

00:01:56.711 --> 00:01:57.996
- I'll just leave this here on your plate.

00:01:57.996 --> 00:01:59.627
- That pork rib is perfectly sized

00:01:59.627 --> 00:02:01.700
and that fat on it is just delicious.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.732
Mm, can't get better than that pork fat.

00:02:03.732 --> 00:02:06.718
- This is delicious, but I don't think

00:02:06.718 --> 00:02:08.080
anything compares to Korean barbecue.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:11.492
- It's definitely good
enough to eat more than once.

00:02:11.492 --> 00:02:12.528
- Here's the thing.

00:02:12.528 --> 00:02:14.500
In the world, Southern
United States barbecue

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.820
and Korean barbecue are the best.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.848
You gather around like
the pit or the grill

00:02:18.848 --> 00:02:21.266
and you have like a cookout or smokeout

00:02:21.266 --> 00:02:23.777
and you have all the like
neighborhood people gather.

00:02:23.777 --> 00:02:26.886
It's like more than just a
cuisine, it's a tradition.

00:02:26.886 --> 00:02:28.104
Look, you're still going for it.

00:02:28.104 --> 00:02:31.384
- Yeah, and I just ate lunch.
(chuckling)

